intro-1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.000
<v Connor>I want to talk about why most product roadmaps are fiction.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:10.200
<v Connor>Every quarter we publish a roadmap with twelve items and we ship maybe five of them.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:15.900
<v Connor>Customers read the roadmap as a promise, and sales reads it as a contract.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.500
<v Connor>So when seven items slip, everyone feels lied to, even though nobody lied.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:27.000
<v Connor>The roadmap was a guess, but we dressed it up as a plan with dates and colours.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:32.800
<v Connor>What if the public roadmap only listed problems we are working on, not features and dates?

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:38.000
<v Connor>Customers could vote on the problems, and we could explain what we learned each month.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:43.400
<v Connor>Sales would hate it at first, because they like having a date to point at in a deal.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:49.100
<v Connor>But a date that slips costs more trust than no date at all, and sales knows that too.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:55.000
<v Connor>I think the honest roadmap is a list of problems, a list of bets, and a monthly letter about what changed.

00:00:55.100 --> 00:01:00.400
<v Connor>That is harder to write than a feature list, but it is the only version customers believe.
